{"reference":"Psalms 72","book":"Psalms","chapter":72,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Book Boundary","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Psalm 72 ends with a doxology—words of praise to God—in verses 18–19, followed by the note that “the prayers of David” are ended. In the Masoretic arrangement of the Psalter, Psalm 72 stands at the end of the second of five books, each marked by a doxological boundary. This helps explain why the chapter closes with praise that broadens from the king’s reign to God’s glory filling the whole earth.","verse_refs":["Psalms 72:1","Psalms 72:18-20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_psalms_072_052e7412f4d4aa0e5fcd9224"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}